cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  Cite this repository, by its Zenodo version DOI, when what you used was the data
  or the code. If you are citing the argument, the method or a result, cite the
  paper instead: "An optically independent administrative reference for validating
  built-surface products, and the tree-canopy bias it reveals" by Devan Cantrell
  Addison-Turner, prepared for GIScience & Remote Sensing. The paper is not yet
  published, so it is deliberately not set as preferred-citation here; see the
  comment below the keywords for why, and the README for both citation forms.
title: "An optically independent administrative reference for validating built-surface products, and the tree-canopy bias it reveals"
abstract: >-
  A validation reference for built-surface products constructed from
  administrative property records rather than imagery. Assessor floor area
  divided by storey count, plus the recorded garage, summed over the parcels in
  a 30 m cell and taken as a share of cell area, gives a lower bound on the
  impervious fraction that cell holds. Because no step uses imagery, the bound
  cannot inherit the occlusion it is used to detect. Across 21,931 parcels in
  Nevada County, California, closed canopy is associated with 18 to 22
  percentage points less measured NLCD impervious surface than open ground.
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Addison-Turner"
    given-names: "Devan Cantrell"
    email: "daddisonturner@stanford.edu"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2511-3680"
    affiliation: "Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Stanford University"
    city: "Stanford"
    region: "California"
    country: "US"
repository-code: "https://github.com/devanaddisonturner/Canopy-Bias-Nevada"

# `version` and `date-released` are here because without them the formatted
# citation has no year. That was the state of this file until 29 September 2026:
# GitHub's widget rendered "Addison-Turner D.C. An optically independent
# administrative reference ... URL: ..." with nothing datable in it, which is not a
# citation anyone can use in a reference list.
#
# Both are set at deposit time and must match the Zenodo release. Update them when
# the deposit is cut; `make_tables.py` refuses a date in the future, so a forgotten
# update fails rather than publishing a date that has not happened.
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-09-30"
keywords:
  - "impervious surface"
  - "accuracy assessment"
  - "tree canopy"
  - "cadastral and assessor data"
  - "NLCD"
  - "validation reference"
license: MIT
license-url: "https://github.com/devanaddisonturner/Canopy-Bias-Nevada/blob/main/LICENSE"

# THE PAPER THIS REPOSITORY ACCOMPANIES, AND WHY IT IS NOT preferred-citation YET
#
# GitHub renders `preferred-citation` INSTEAD OF the top-level entry when one is
# present; its own documentation says so. So a preferred-citation here would make
# the "Cite this repository" button show an unpublished manuscript carrying no DOI,
# no URL and no version: a citation with nothing a reader can resolve. It would
# also hide the Zenodo DOI, which lives on the top-level entry, from the one place
# a visitor looks for it.
#
# Until the paper is published, the button therefore shows the REPOSITORY, which is
# the thing a visitor actually has in front of them and can resolve. The README
# tells readers to cite the paper when they are citing the argument, and gives both
# forms. The paper's details are kept here, commented out, so that restoring them is
# an uncommenting rather than a rewrite:
#
# preferred-citation:
#   type: article                 # `unpublished` until it is accepted
#   title: "An optically independent administrative reference for validating built-surface products, and the tree-canopy bias it reveals"
#   authors:
#     - family-names: "Addison-Turner"
#       given-names: "Devan Cantrell"
#       email: "daddisonturner@stanford.edu"
#       orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2511-3680"
#       affiliation: "Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Stanford University"
#   year: 2026
#   journal: "GIScience & Remote Sensing"
#   doi: 10.xxxx/xxxxx            # the ARTICLE doi, not the Zenodo one
#
# ON ACCEPTANCE: uncomment the block, fill in the journal, volume, issue, pages and
# the article DOI, and delete the `type: article` comment. At that point the paper
# is resolvable and citing it from the button is both correct and useful.
# make_tables.py checks the states against each other, so this cannot be forgotten
# quietly: once the manuscript's DOI placeholder is gone, the harness fails until
# this file names the published article.

# The datasets in this repository are separately dedicated to the public domain
# under CC0 1.0; see the second half of LICENSE.
#
# The Zenodo DOI below is the VERSION DOI for v1.0.0, reserved on 30 September
# 2026 before the files were built, so that the archive at that DOI states its own
# DOI. It is not the concept DOI: a concept DOI resolves to whichever version is
# newest, which is the wrong target for a citation of the deposit someone ran.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23072749
